  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In July 2021 the Marshall Islands Chamber of Commerce (MICC) adjusted its organizational structure in response to the changing business environment and organizational needs by launching a digital secretariat with platform for membership engagement. To provide independent secretariat support that is easy and efficient for members to access without need for a physical office and staffing, the digital secretariat adopted by the Chamber takes the familiar form of a website managed directly by members of the Chambers executive committee. The launch of the platform introduced a new working method for the Chamber. Using this tool, the Chamber is able to engage with members digitally to provide them with information on policy and regulations, regular updates on activities and events, access to training opportunities via the Pacific eCampus for employer and business membership organizations, sharing practical tools and guidance for business, and giving members insight into the governance of the Chamber. For businesses interested in joining the MICC, the Chamber is using the digital platform to present the value of membership and provides access to a digital form for applying. Behind this public interface, the back end of the website provides a platform for the Chamber to ensure board members have access to relevant information needed to perform their role and support membership engagement. Without a physical secretariat but aiming to grow membership among youth entrepreneurs, the Chamber made a calculated decision to launch a digital platform as the demographic of youth entrepreneurs is more responsive and comfortable with online engagement. The Chamber also incorporated into the platform profiles of 25 young entrepreneurs and built an online business directory of young entrepreneurs where members can promote their products and services. As a result of this initiative, 26 youth entrepreneurs have taken up membership in the Chamber. In collaboration with the ITC-ILO and Pacific Island Private Sector Organization (PIPSO), the ILO delivered a training to the Chamber in October 2020 on Good Governance and a second training on Effective Membership Strategies in November 2020 with 75% of the Chamber board participated in both courses alongside 33 representatives of other EBMO in the Pacific. Following the training, the ILO provided technical assistance to the Chamber for the development of a membership strategy prioritizing youth entrepreneurs and technical scoping of the website to implement the strategy. The ILO provided assistance for the design and structure of the digital platform and profiles of entrepreneurs, and on a cost-sharing basis, supported the development costs of the website and the production of entrepreneur profiles and feature articles. Further work to support this result included the launch of a Pacific eCampus for Pacific employer and business membership organizations in May 2021 with a designated user account for the Chamber.</narrative>
   </description>
